        heading: 'Influencer Discovery and Vetting',
        body: `Influencer discovery is the process of identifying creators whose audience characteristics, content quality, and brand fit make them suitable partners for specific marketing objectives. The scale of the creator landscape — millions of active creators across major platforms — makes systematic discovery tools necessary for efficiency, and rigorous vetting processes necessary for quality control.

Influencer discovery platforms (AspireIQ, Grin, CreatorIQ, Upfluence, Later Influence) provide searchable databases of creator profiles with audience demographic data, engagement metrics, content examples, and contact information. Search filters include: platform, follower range, engagement rate, audience location, audience age and gender demographics, content category, and audience interest topics. These platforms significantly reduce the time required for initial discovery compared to manual search, but require human judgment in evaluating suitability beyond the metrics.

Audience demographic analysis is the first quality check after discovery. The relevant questions: What percentage of the creator's audience is in the target geographic market? (A creator with 200,000 followers may have 60% in Brazil for a brand that only ships domestically in the UK — that creator's UK audience is only 80,000.) What percentage matches the target age and gender demographic? (A fitness creator whose audience skews heavily male is not the right partner for a women's activewear brand even if fitness is the shared category.) Are the audience's interest categories aligned with the product's use case? Answering these questions using the audience data provided by discovery platforms or by requesting a creator's analytics screenshots directly prevents investing in partnerships that reach large numbers of the wrong people.

Engagement quality assessment goes beyond the engagement rate percentage to evaluate the nature of the engagement. A creator with a 5% engagement rate on posts that receive mostly generic emoji comments and single-word responses has lower engagement quality than a creator with a 3% rate whose comments include detailed questions, personal stories, and substantive discussion. Tools like HypeAuditor and SparkToro provide engagement quality scoring, identifying accounts with suspicious follower and engagement patterns. Manual review of recent comment threads is the most reliable engagement quality check.

Fraud detection is a necessary step in influencer vetting because purchased followers and manufactured engagement are widespread. Indicators of fraudulent audience growth: unusually high follower counts relative to the account's age or content posting history, engagement rates far above the platform average (which can indicate purchased engagement), sudden follower spikes followed by declines (indicating mass unfollowing after a purchased follower delivery), and follower quality issues (low-quality follower accounts with no profile photos, no posts, and suspicious usernames). Influencer fraud detection tools apply algorithmic analysis to follower and engagement patterns to identify likely fraud. Brands that skip fraud detection discover the problem after paying for partnerships that reach fake audiences, which is an expensive way to learn.

Past brand partnership review evaluates whether the creator has worked with competing brands, brands that conflict with the brand's values, or brands whose audience might negatively associate with the target brand. Reviewing a creator's sponsored content history (visible through tools or manual feed review) identifies conflicts before partnership discussions begin. Creators who regularly accept any sponsorship regardless of relevance to their content signal to their audience that their recommendations are entirely commercial — which reduces the credibility value of their endorsements. Creators who are selective about partnerships and maintain genuine thematic coherence in their sponsored content have more credible endorsement value.`
